'use strict';
window.PLACE_STORIES={
  gyeongbok:{title:'A palace that kept returning',theme:'Power, ceremony & resilience',chapters:[
    {title:'01 · A new dynasty needs a home',text:'Before you take another photograph, pause and look at the scale of the palace grounds. This was a statement about a new beginning. The Joseon dynasty was founded in 1392, and Gyeongbokgung followed in 1395 as its principal palace. Think of this place as the public face of a new political order, rather than simply a royal home. The open spaces and repeated gateways encourage you to slow down. As you look toward the main buildings, notice how the architecture makes the center feel important. That is a useful way to read a palace: ask what it wants you to notice, and what it keeps out of sight.'},
    {title:'02 · The surprising gap in its story',text:'There is a twist behind this seemingly timeless scene. The palace was destroyed during the Japanese invasion of 1592. It was not promptly rebuilt. More than two and a half centuries passed before the major reconstruction completed in 1867. Later destruction during the colonial period was followed by modern restoration. So the palace before you is not an untouched survival from one moment in the past. It is a place that has repeatedly been lost, rebuilt and recovered. Looking at it that way changes the visit: you are seeing not only royal ambition, but the work of remembering.'},
    {title:'03 · Two different faces of royal life',text:'Keep two names in mind: Geunjeongjeon and Gyeonghoeru. Geunjeongjeon, the great ceremonial hall, was used for occasions such as coronations and receiving foreign envoys. Its name connects kingship with diligent government. Imagine the formality of an audience here; that imagined scene helps explain the imposing scale. Gyeonghoeru presents another face of the court. This pavilion was a setting for royal banquets and entertaining envoys beside the pond. Look at the relationship between water and architecture. One setting projects authority, while the other creates an atmosphere for hospitality. Together, they make the palace feel less like a collection of buildings and more like a stage for different kinds of royal life.'}
  ],sources:[{title:'Korea Heritage Service · Palace history',url:'https://royal.khs.go.kr/ENG/contents/E101010000.do'},{title:'Visit Seoul · Palace halls and royal ceremonies',url:'https://english.visitseoul.net/PalaceArea/GyeongbokgungPalace/ENN000608'}]},
  bukchon:{title:'Old roofs, a surprisingly modern story',theme:'Homes, craft & a changing city',chapters:[{title:'01 · Look beyond the postcard',text:'Look along the roofline for a moment. Bukchon can feel like a neighborhood preserved outside time, but its story is more interesting than that. Its location between major royal sites connects it to the older city. Yet many of the tightly packed hanok streets took their present form during the twentieth century, when larger plots were divided into smaller homes. That contrast is part of the appeal: traditional building forms adapted to a city that was changing.'},{title:'02 · A neighborhood, not a stage set',text:'Seoul’s accounts of Bukchon highlight the developer Jeong Se-kwon and the urban hanok built in the 1920s and 1930s. These houses belonged to the life of a growing city, not simply to an imagined royal past. As you look at the thresholds, walls and roof edges, think about how a home creates privacy in a narrow street. The most meaningful detail here is easy to overlook: people still live behind these doors. Enjoy the view quietly. The neighborhood’s continuing everyday life is part of its history.'}],sources:[{title:'Seoul Metropolitan Government · Bukchon history',url:'https://english.seoul.go.kr/seoul-opens-bukchon-hanok-history-center/'},{title:'Seoul Hanok · The changing neighborhood',url:'https://hanok.seoul.go.kr/m/eng/town/town01.do'}]},
  louvre:{title:'Before the paintings, a fortress',theme:'Kings, revolution & reinvention',chapters:[{title:'01 · Read the building first',text:'Before thinking about the paintings, let the building itself become the first exhibit. The Louvre began as a fortress under Philip Augustus around 1190. Its first purpose was protection, not the display of art. Later generations transformed and enlarged it into a royal palace. Try holding those two ideas together as you look around: a defensive stronghold becoming a place where rulers represented their power.'},{title:'02 · A different kind of audience',text:'In 1793, during the French Revolution, the Louvre opened as a museum. That date changes the meaning of the place. A former royal setting acquired a new public role. Today it is easy to rush straight toward a famous work. Instead, spare a moment for the courtyards and the scale of the wings. The building tells a long story of changing authority before you have even entered a gallery.'}],sources:[{title:'Musée du Louvre · Palace and collection history',url:'https://presse.louvre.fr/le-palais-et-ses-collections/?lang=fr'}]},
  orsay:{title:'When this gallery was a railway station',theme:'Modern life & a second act',chapters:[{title:'01 · Imagine an arrival',text:'Take a moment to imagine this space filled with travelers instead of museum visitors. The Gare d’Orsay opened for the 1900 World’s Fair and served trains arriving from southwestern France. Looking at the great interior, you can still approach it as an architecture of arrival: a place built to make entering Paris feel significant.'},{title:'02 · The building became the collection’s frame',text:'The station eventually gained a different purpose. The Musée d’Orsay opened to the public in December 1986. Its transformation kept a remarkable railway building in use while creating a setting for art. That gives your visit two layers. There are the works you have come to see, and there is the building that surrounds them. Look up occasionally. Here, the frame around the art has a story of its own.'}],sources:[{title:'Musée d’Orsay · History of the museum',url:'https://www.musee-orsay.fr/fr/articles/histoire-du-musee-60'}]},
  eiffel:{title:'The landmark that needed a reason to stay',theme:'Engineering, experiments & radio',chapters:[{title:'01 · Paris was not promised this view forever',text:'It is difficult to picture Paris without this tower. Yet when it opened for the 1889 World’s Fair, its future was not guaranteed. Gustave Eiffel’s concession lasted twenty years. So the question was not only how to build something extraordinary, but how to give it a lasting purpose.'},{title:'02 · An invisible part of the story',text:'Scientific experiments and radio communication helped provide that purpose. The tower became useful in ways a visitor cannot see just by looking at its iron structure. Its height made it valuable for transmitting signals, and that usefulness helped secure its survival. Now look upward again. The familiar silhouette is only half the story. The other half traveled invisibly through the air.'}],sources:[{title:'Official Eiffel Tower website · How radio helped save the tower',url:'https://www.toureiffel.paris/en/news/130-years/why-was-eiffel-tower-kept'}]},
  tuileries:{title:'A royal garden becomes a public pause',theme:'Landscape & ways of seeing',chapters:[{title:'01 · A garden designed to be seen',text:'Before choosing a chair, look down the long lines of the garden. The Tuileries grew from a royal setting into a public park. Parts of the formal garden nearest the palace were designed to be appreciated from its windows. That is a different way of imagining a garden: as a composition seen from above, as well as a place experienced on foot.'},{title:'02 · Make time part of the visit',text:'Notice how paths, open spaces and planting organize your view. Then notice how people use the same setting today: a conversation, a quiet seat, a pause between museums. You do not need to hurry toward the next monument to experience the city. Here, the history is partly in the layout, and partly in the way a once-royal landscape became a shared everyday space.'}],sources:[{title:'Musée du Louvre · From royal garden to public park',url:'https://www.louvre.fr/en/explore/the-palace/from-royal-garden-to-public-park'}]}
};
